A||||15||Bluebottlejunky||need advice?  ||||10||
Z||000000||Bluebottlejunky||04-21-2001||03:52 PM||||ok im 15 i have tons of freinds all are diffrent in there own ways..my close ones there is like 5 of them,  and yes some of them do drugs/smoke/drink and now my mom thinks im some druggy when im really not..?  What can I do..i mean i have tried it once and decided I dont like it. Its really annoying too..I just feel really bad..<BR><P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>85'GT 4sp, <BR>AOL IM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||10
Z||000001||Shaft||04-21-2001||05:01 PM||||I had this same problem a few years ago. I wouldn't worry about it. I just tried to ignore what my parents thought (mostly my mom) and if they asked me something about it I told them the truth and stood by my position. It didn't hurt my relationship with my parents and we still have a close family.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bluebottlejunky||04-21-2001||05:58 PM||||ya I dunno she says all the time I should get new freinds..but I mean my freinds arent that bad at all..And Im never gunna give up my freinds.  Thanks for your advice Shaft.<P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>85'GT 4sp, <BR>AOL IM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||1
Z||000003||hugh||04-21-2001||09:51 PM||||Mike,Your mother is like every other.She doesn't want you to be around people who may influence you to do the wrong things.You may not,or you may in a weak moment,do just what you don't think you will do now.She has your best interests at heart.Be really honest with yourself and think,how you would feel if your roles were reversed?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||04-21-2001||11:08 PM||||It's sad, but we often find ourselves becoming like our friends. I used to know a guy who was drunk and stoned all the time. He was probably one of the nicest guys ya'd ever want to meet. But I could only be around him so much, then I would have to seperate myself, because I knew it would be too easy to be dragged into that stuff. (And I have an iron will) Be honest, what do you really have in common with these "friends" that you couldn't have in common with others, that might actually be more like you? I tend to call things as I see 'em, and I'd be willing to almost bet two things: I bet you hold on to these friends one, because you like that your mom worries, might even be a defiance thing, and two, you probably like the temptation. We all like to walk up to the edge and just peek over once in a while. Those things are normal if they're true, but probably niether is really in your best interest.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cameron||04-21-2001||11:35 PM||||Personally, I think it is not the friends we keep it is the things we do that should matter.  Parents are a pain in the butt.  Just wait until you move out and then party. hehe.  But really, I have friends that do drugs and smoke and hell I even drink, but then again I am a little bit older, but not enough to make a difference.  Parents never will understand kids.  Ya ya they've been there and all but they either have forgoten, don't know what it is like for kids now days, or have turned into their parents.  Just don't sweat it.  Hold your ground and tell your parents that you aren't changing your friends but tell them the truth.  Tell them you don't do anything.  I had to tell my parents that I'm not changing my friends just because they have some bad habits.  And sometimes you can turn the conversation around and get them defensive by asking them "Do you think I am not strong willed?" but make sure it's at a point when you defended your position really well.  If they say "no" then you can say then why can't I keep my friends.  If they say "yes" then say "what you don't believe in me?"  Hehe just make sure that you control the conversation.  Lead their words around until you have them right where you want them.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good luck<P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bluebottlejunky||04-21-2001||11:52 PM||||man its killing me, this are my freinds from like 1st grade it has been so long...it not like 24/7 they are stoned but they do it..It just dosen't seem right that i can leave all my freinds.  I can picture my self as my mother...but I guess my freinds are just trying to act cool. thanks for your help guys<P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>85'GT 4sp, <BR>AOL IM: Fierofanatic2001||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||04-22-2001||10:25 AM||||One other thing to consider. Suppose that you and all of your friends are together. They're stoned. You're not. You all get pulled over. The police do a search and find pot in the car. Nobody claims it. The police "might" call it community property, and charge everybody. Think your friends are "stand-up" enough to not let you get dragged into it? I wouldn't bet on it.<P>Having said all that... I probably would continue to associate. At least a little bit. I'd just watch my ass really carefully. Don't allow yourself to be put in a situation that may not be good for your well-being. Whatever that situation may be. <BR>Think.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000008||Old Lar||04-22-2001||10:49 AM||||What some of the others have said: guilt by assocition. Peer pressure.  In my younger days, we drank under age and I puked my guts out a few times, and had some of the worse hangovers, so I stopped that. I still get bad hangoves even from very little alcohol.<P>Drugs, I never tried, they were coming on big time in the late 60's, but I was a medic in Viet-Nam, and if you were caught doing drugs, that was 3 months bad time, and you got to make up your good time in Viet-Nam.  Not a fun place to be,  and I wanted out.  Fear of the punishment was a deterrent.<P>You will do what you want to do, no matter what anyone tells you.  You will have to live with the consequences.  The only thing you have to do in this world is to die. Any other choice you make has some sort of consequence that you may not like.<P>One of the responsibilities of parents (if they are any good) is to give you guidence from their lifes experiences.  Too many parents give up, and let their children do as they please and the child makes poor decisions.||||reg||1
Z||000009||KRMFiero||04-22-2001||12:09 PM||||I was just thinking about the car thing, what if say    I got pulled over and someone was high in my car, he had like a pipe on him but i didnt know it and he told the cop i didnt know it   would i get introuble?<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000010||Old Lar||04-22-2001||05:04 PM||||Trouble!  Probably you would be hasseled, cuffed and until the explanation is believed hasseled some more.  If you were lucky, you'd spend four or five hours explaining your vesion of the siuation.  Depending on the guilty party, whether he backs you or says you were in on it all along.<P>Put yourself in a cops positon, some kids driving around and one is stoned and the driver or other passengers don't know about it?   Sure officer, I thought that the tobacco smelled different, but I didn't inhale.<BR>A good way to lose your car.||||reg||1
Z||000011||My7Fieros||04-24-2001||02:52 AM||||Bluebottle sounds like a typical 15 year old...but he does have good respect for his parents (did that come out right?)....well, you know what I mean.<BR>Sometimes kids are only gonna learn by expierience. I thought the same kinds of things when I was 15 (which would've been in 1987). <B>Belive me</B>, there is little to no difference in now and 1987 as far as teenagers are concerned. 1987 and 1957? Yes, but not much has changed in the past 14 or so years, so I know Im not THAT much outta touch. Where the hell am I going with all of this?.....I dunno, now Im confusing myself!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR>Oh yeah, keep your friends unless they are persistant on persuadiing you to do things you dont want to do, and be honest with your parents. Um, just say no to drugs!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Man Im tired, Im going to bed before I confuse anyone else lol. M7F||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cameron||04-24-2001||04:33 AM||||I've never been caught in the stituation, but if the cops thought you were in on it and your friends didn't back you up...ya you would be in alot of trouble.  If your friends stood up for you however, they would probaly just let you off and leave you and take your friend.  I was caught once with a consiled hand gun in my dad's truck, I was driving, if that makes sence.  My dad stood up for me and said I had no knowledge of it and I made sure they knew it was his truck and I was only driving it.  They took him to the station to get all his info and stayed in jail over night.  I guess I'm glad I didn't know about it ... that could have sucked.  The stituation is a little different there but the outcome would be the same if your story was backed up.  But, personally I think it would be easier to avoid the stituation.  By all means see your friends.  Nobody can or should take them away from you, but careful being in the car with them.  Also, make sure you and your friends have an understanding that if anything happens to make sure cops know that you have nothing to do with it.  A good friend would be happy to keep you out of it.  I know that I would go down for my friends to save their neck.<P>Any who, wish you luck with your parents and all.  Hope I could help<P><P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||rogergarrison||04-24-2001||09:19 AM||||I was raised in the 'free love, pot' era. Ive always distanced myself from anyone who used drugs. Kept me out of trouble all these years not to mention all the thousands of dollars I saved (to build my dream car). Even though you think there your friends, theyll turn on you in a second to keep themselves out of trouble or get the next high. My advice is loose them, or at most keep just a casual relationship with them. As far as the parents thing, just keep it honest with them and youll have no regrets.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||JSocha||04-24-2001||11:46 AM||||Bluebottle,<P>I have to agree with "rogergarrison" and some others and have to disagree with what I will take us people more your age in their comments.<P>I myself come from an extremly disfunctional family, drugs and alcohol (older brothers, father/mother).<P>Have you ever heard the statement "Its hard to sore like an Eagle when your hanging with a bunch of Turkeys"?<P>No matter how good of friends you may feel they are now, they will not be there or they will double cross you (this depending on how deep they are into the scene) when there a$$e$ are in the proverbial sling.  How do I know?  Been there...done that!  <P>I personally have a strong constitution myself and have never taken drugs (at least with my knowledge as I will elaborate here shortly).<P>My step-daughter, now 20 and living on her own, is just starting to learn that herself after my wife and I got married a couple of years ago and where she has really turned around.  She hung around the drug dependent alcholics and was only going to live until she was 18 and thought her friends were cool.<P>I layed it straight to her as I knew she thought smoking the "jane" was cool where she was going to be by hanging around them and what they were going to do.  Been there...<P>All of her "druggie" friends are being arrested here in the small town of Rugby and are doing 8 to 10 in the state pen.<P>She's been living on her own for 1 1/2 years in Minot, she just got busted and her so called friends, didn't stick up for her and she took the fall, even her "closest of close" friends!<P>Last night she came home unexpecdedly and ased if she could move home over the summer, so that she can get away from some of the people she has been hanging around with and go back to college.<P>Its was all fine and dandy when her closest friends could benefit, but they had a scape goat when they need it and my step daughter was it.<P>BTW, she now frowns on drugs and stays away from many of her older friends.<P>There is a saying, "you are who you hang out with".<P>Now, off of her and onto my personal experience.  I stated I never took drugs at least not that I knew of.<P>When I was younger and seen what was happening to my brothers (one in jail most of his life the other burned out where he now is on disability for the rest of his life and scares the heck out of me and my now aquired family as he is now a schitzophrinic due to the drugs) and felt the same way about their friends, that they would never get into that scene, but they did.  There "so called" good friends eventually rubbed off on them.  <P>So, since I seen this happen when I was growing up, I promised myself I would never smoke, drink or take drugs as I seen what it was doing to my parents, family and especially my brothers.  <P>Even though, I personally hung around people that did the exact same thing as yours, drank, smoked the weed and took other drugs, even cocaine.  <P>Eventually I started to drink just to be "in" when I was with them and not be seen as a potential outcast when we would all go out to parties.  <P>Then I went to college and the drinking, well, lets just say I had a 4.0 on the alcohol meter and a 0.67 grade average, but at the same time I was hanging around people that drank, did drugs, smoked, you name it bluebottle, they did it.<P>Partied nightly which also started to become a daily routine, and again, to be "in" I started to smoke (that thankfully lasted a month and my constitution kicked in and I haven't smoked since).  <P>Eventually, I flunked out of college and came home after 5 wasted years.  Again, I hung around yet kept what I felt was a fair distance from people that were doing the drugs, alchohol and smoked.<P>Now I'm at home, small town to which I come from (less then 3,000 pop), most of my so called friends, most of them now somewhat heavier into drugs (when it was what you could consider mild in our school days) and alchohol were either now dealing or regular users and I hung around them, because after all they were my friends.<P>I didn't approve of what they did, but they were friends from my high school days so though I stayed my relative distance inside and yet still went out and partied with them.  <P>Of course, when they got in trouble and I was around with them, didn't matter, I got in trouble too and they never backed me.<P>Young and dumb (all old people are that way at one time too...its no different today then it was yester-year), I continued to hang around them even when I felt I was keeping what I felt was my safe distance.<P>When they got in trouble...so did I.  They invited me to parties and I invited them.<P>As I stated, I never took drugs personally.<P>My so called high school friends, who I was hanging around with, even though they knew I disapproved of their behavior in high school and today and to that day who I thought respected my decision not to take drugs, eventually turned on me without my knowledge.<P>When we were out bar-hopping, parties, or the like, my so called friends of many, many years who I felt respected my decision not to take drugs, started to put drugs into my drinks.<P>Without me knowing it, it got so bad I didn't know if I was coming or going; was halucinating at work; didn't know where I was or how I got to places; couldn't sleep (because I was being fed speed and downers) and tried to kill myself back in August of 1994 by taking 12 bottles of sleeping pills because all I wanted to do was get some sleep and I was feeling really, really weird.<P>I didn't know any better at that point and when I started to hear kids sing as I was going to sleep which was very relaxing...well, I lived out in the country with no neighbors for two miles in either direction you figure it out.<P>My girlfriend luckily at that time found me, I ended up in the psych ward that night and then in a alchohol/drug reabillitation center where I personally learned I had meth, speed, LSD, qualudes, valuim and other drugs in my system.  How did it get there????  My constitution was strong and I knew it wasn't from me taking them personally...then it hit me!  My friends I have been hanging around with.<P>Here I am in a drug and alchohol reabilitation center having to admit to something I "didn't" do personally.  In the meantime, my so called friends, back home telling everyone I've been taking drugs for years and getting stoned with them are laughing it up.  Something to this day I'm still trying to shed as far as the image my so called friends have graciously given to me!  I became their scap goat and was the bunt of their sick and twisted joke which nearly killed me.<P>Have you ever been in a rehab?  Hope not...because it took me over two months just to get out the rehab's door (because they did not believe a word I said about not taking the drugs) and I had to be sponsered and go to regular AA meetings (and thats not Athletics Anonymous for most of you where when you get the urge to run, you call a few friends and they rush over with a 6 pack and talk you out of it) where several of the members after about a month, month and a half during a weekly AA meeting told me I didn't belong in the group, got me back up to the rehab center and told the coucilors that I should have first not been in rehab and second my sponser felt that although I had issues, I didn't need to be in AA and that he personally believed me that I did not know about the drugs being given to me.<P>I think I have gotten off the main topic a bit, sorry.<P>I am certain your parents believe you that you don't take drugs, yet they are concerned of how long this may last.<P>You may have personally a strong constitution against it, but where do your friends true constitutions, respect and convictions lay on your beliefs now?  Even theres?  May be there now, but who knows what will happen in the future.  This will be dependent on how deep they get into this scene and really how screwed up they get.<P>As far as my brothers go, they thought the same and now my one brother, who has a family of his own, gets straight, then starts hanging around his "crony" buddies and lapses back into their life styles.  Just caught him again several months ago smoking weed even though he tells everyone he is done with it and is straight.<P>After awhile, do you think anyone is going to believe what he says?  I don't and he is my brother...<P>You need to ask yourself personally "where do I want to be and how successfull?".  <P>"Who is it that I may hang out with now...that may either influence my life positively or draw or take me down with them today or in the future?"<P>Bluebottle, I'm a newbie to this post and I have read alot of threads where you have responded or been responded to by others, and I see the comradre and friendship that "you" have gained here.  The outsider looking in and telling you what I see.<P>Why not take advantage of that?<P>I can't tell you what to do and neither can anyone else.  They can only make suggestions to which in the end, you can either take or leave or adapt some of it into your knowledge base.<P>My personal recommendation to you if I may, is don't loose touch with those that you consider are your friends now that are in conflict with your parents due to their drug background, but keep a very good distance from them for now and get friends who have the same belief's and more in common with you.<P>Over time, you may never know that one or several of these friends may need your "personal" help in the future where they thought you have forgotten them, where possibly their other friends have, to get straight or to get their lives back on track and may need your support and strength as a friend to do so.  There you may find you could have a stronger bond to them later where all becomes positive, for you and for them!  <P>Also remember, you are who you hang out with.  <P>You may not do the same things they do, but others will and tend to judge you personally via "guilt by association".  ie, They do it, so therefore you must as well.<P>Ask yourself, have you ever judge someone by the way they look, what you have heard about them or who they have hung out with?  I would be very surprised and amazed if you yourself said "no".<P>We are human after all and we tend to do what everyone else does.<P>On a final note, as I'm probably over half your age as well as many others here on this post/forum.  I ask that you all who maybe in either in your teens or even over 50 to keep this in mind "You are never to old, to grow up".  You will be doing this for the rest of your life...!  Sad, isn't it?<P>And, you may feel that us being "old a$$e$/farts" that we have forgotten what it is or was like to be a teen, but the opposite is true.  <P>We've been there, we've done it, been down the same round you're going down, seen the same things and we can see what may "possibly" happen based on circumstances that we have see and can relate to.  <P>As parent's (me an instant parent as of 3 years ago of three teenage daughters) we are drawing on our past experiences, our personal lives, to guide you so that you make the most of your life and are successful.  You will do it one day too.<P>How do I know?<P>Simple, when you come home to your future house; spouse and/or kids with a hefty mortgage one day and ask "Who the he!! left these d@mn lights on again?" and suddenly turn around quickly and say "DAD!?!?!?!", you'll then know you're one of us "old farts" that you may be calling your parents now!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Whew...I'm shutting up so that any other elders and friends to bluebottle out here can throw their 2cents in!<P>Sorry, I'm a man long on words, but short on explanation!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jnjsochasig.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 04-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||hugh||04-24-2001||07:00 PM||||Jeff,That was impressive.I applaud you on your honesty and willingness to bare your feelings to help someone!||||reg||1
